1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,527
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,527 --> 00:00:04,542
In this lecture we study Vector Spaces: A Short Introduction.

3
00:00:04,542 --> 00:00:07,175
Generated with course2video (prototype).

4
00:00:07,775 --> 00:00:09,063
Here is the plan.

5
00:00:09,063 --> 00:00:14,593
We will go through 7 parts: Introduction, Vectors in R n, Definition of a

6
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:22,244
vector space, Subspaces, Linear combinations and span, Linear independence and basis and Exercise and

7
00:00:22,244 --> 00:00:22,926
solution.

8
00:00:23,526 --> 00:00:26,394
Let's start with part 1: Introduction.

9
00:00:26,994 --> 00:00:30,401
Let's begin with a quick overview of where we're headed.

10
00:00:30,401 --> 00:00:34,965
This is an introduction to vector spaces, aimed at a first course in linear

11
00:00:34,965 --> 00:00:35,452
algebra.

12
00:00:35,452 --> 00:00:39,894
We'll start with vectors in R n, the most concrete setting, and then move

13
00:00:39,894 --> 00:00:43,058
to the axioms that define a vector space in general.

14
00:00:43,058 --> 00:00:48,352
From there we'll look at subspaces, which are vector spaces sitting inside larger ones.

15
00:00:48,352 --> 00:00:53,281
After that come three closely linked ideas: span, linear independence, and bases.

16
00:00:53,281 --> 00:00:57,601
Together they let us describe a space efficiently and measure its size.

17
00:00:57,601 --> 00:01:02,469
Finally, we'll close with a short exercise and walk through its solution, so you

18
00:01:02,469 --> 00:01:06,851
can check your understanding of everything we've built up along the way.

19
00:01:07,451 --> 00:01:10,899
Now we move on to part 2: Vectors in R n.

20
00:01:11,499 --> 00:01:17,129
With that roadmap in mind, let's start with the most familiar example: vectors in

21
00:01:17,129 --> 00:01:17,407
R n.

22
00:01:17,407 --> 00:01:21,577
A vector in R n is simply an ordered list of n real numbers,

23
00:01:21,577 --> 00:01:24,009
written u equals u one through u n.

24
00:01:24,009 --> 00:01:29,639
Addition is done component by component: u plus v equals the vector whose entries

25
00:01:29,639 --> 00:01:32,489
are u one plus v one, up to u n plus v n.

26
00:01:32,489 --> 00:01:35,825
In other words, we add corresponding components.

27
00:01:35,825 --> 00:01:41,038
Scalar multiplication works similarly: a times u equals a u one through a u

28
00:01:41,038 --> 00:01:45,486
n, so multiplying by a scalar a multiplies every component by a.

29
00:01:45,486 --> 00:01:49,447
The zero vector is the one whose components are all zero.

30
00:01:49,447 --> 00:01:53,896
And in R two and R three, we can picture vectors as arrows drawn

31
00:01:53,896 --> 00:01:57,788
from the origin, which gives useful geometric intuition.

32
00:01:58,388 --> 00:02:02,823
Now we move on to part 3: Definition of a vector space.

33
00:02:03,423 --> 00:02:07,785
Now that we've seen how addition and scaling behave in R n, we can

34
00:02:07,785 --> 00:02:11,222
abstract those properties into a general definition.

35
00:02:11,222 --> 00:02:15,320
A real vector space is a set V equipped with an addition and a

36
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:18,625
scalar multiplication that satisfy certain axioms.

37
00:02:18,625 --> 00:02:23,979
Addition must be commutative and associative, so order and grouping don't matter.

38
00:02:23,979 --> 00:02:28,936
There must be a zero vector, and every vector must have an additive inverse

39
00:02:28,936 --> 00:02:30,258
that cancels it out.

40
00:02:30,258 --> 00:02:36,140
Scalar multiplication must distribute both over vector addition and over scalar addition.

41
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:40,635
The equation shows the first of these: a times the quantity u plus v

42
00:02:40,635 --> 00:02:41,957
equals a u plus a v.

43
00:02:41,957 --> 00:02:46,782
Put simply, scaling a sum of vectors gives the same result as summing the

44
00:02:46,782 --> 00:02:47,773
scaled vectors.

45
00:02:48,373 --> 00:02:51,979
Having stated the axioms, let's see what they buy us.

46
00:02:51,979 --> 00:02:56,470
First, R n is itself a vector space, which is the model we started

47
00:02:56,470 --> 00:02:56,811
from.

48
00:02:56,811 --> 00:03:01,982
But the axioms also capture objects that don't look like lists of numbers at

49
00:03:01,982 --> 00:03:02,390
first.

50
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:07,085
Polynomials of degree at most n form a vector space, since we can add

51
00:03:07,085 --> 00:03:09,399
them and multiply them by scalars.

52
00:03:09,399 --> 00:03:13,618
Likewise, the set of all m by n matrices forms a vector space.

53
00:03:13,618 --> 00:03:18,653
This is exactly why the axioms matter: any theorem we prove using only the

54
00:03:18,653 --> 00:03:22,736
axioms automatically holds in all of these examples at once.

55
00:03:22,736 --> 00:03:27,023
We do the work one time and get results for every vector space.

56
00:03:27,623 --> 00:03:31,117
Now we move on to part 4: Subspaces.

57
00:03:31,717 --> 00:03:37,356
Since we now have the general definition of a vector space, a natural next

58
00:03:37,356 --> 00:03:41,471
question is what vector spaces live inside other ones.

59
00:03:41,471 --> 00:03:45,586
A subset W of V is a subspace if it is itself a vector

60
00:03:45,586 --> 00:03:48,025
space using the same operations.

61
00:03:48,025 --> 00:03:51,073
In practice, we check just a few things.

62
00:03:51,073 --> 00:03:53,968
First, W must contain the zero vector.

63
00:03:53,968 --> 00:04:00,751
Second, W must be closed under addition and under scalar multiplication, meaning sums and

64
00:04:00,751 --> 00:04:04,103
scalar multiples of elements of W stay in W.

65
00:04:04,103 --> 00:04:08,676
For example, the line y equals two x in R two is a subspace.

66
00:04:08,676 --> 00:04:13,553
By contrast, the line y equals two x plus one is not, because it

67
00:04:13,553 --> 00:04:16,067
does not contain the zero vector.

68
00:04:16,667 --> 00:04:21,578
Now we move on to part 5: Linear combinations and span.

69
00:04:22,178 --> 00:04:26,967
Subspaces raise the question of how to build them, and that brings us to

70
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:28,896
linear combinations and span.

71
00:04:28,896 --> 00:04:34,083
A linear combination of vectors v one through v k uses scalar coefficients: we

72
00:04:34,083 --> 00:04:37,276
form a one v one plus a two v two, and so on, up

73
00:04:37,276 --> 00:04:38,007
to a k v k.

74
00:04:38,007 --> 00:04:42,929
Each vector is scaled by a coefficient and the results are added together.

75
00:04:42,929 --> 00:04:47,451
The span of a set is the collection of all such linear combinations.

76
00:04:47,451 --> 00:04:51,176
An important fact is that the span is always a subspace.

77
00:04:51,176 --> 00:04:55,765
For a geometric picture, in R three, the span of two vectors that are

78
00:04:55,765 --> 00:05:00,155
not multiples of each other is a plane passing through the origin.

79
00:05:00,755 --> 00:05:05,631
Now we move on to part 6: Linear independence and basis.

80
00:05:06,231 --> 00:05:10,237
Once we know how sets of vectors can span a space, it's natural to

81
00:05:10,237 --> 00:05:13,090
ask whether any of those vectors are redundant.

82
00:05:13,090 --> 00:05:15,518
That is the idea of linear independence.

83
00:05:15,518 --> 00:05:19,707
Vectors v one through v k are linearly independent if the only way to

84
00:05:19,707 --> 00:05:24,017
obtain the zero vector as a linear combination of them is by taking all

85
00:05:24,017 --> 00:05:25,656
coefficients equal to zero.

86
00:05:25,656 --> 00:05:31,058
If some other choice of coefficients also gives zero, the vectors are linearly dependent.

87
00:05:31,058 --> 00:05:35,368
And when they are dependent, at least one of the vectors can be written

88
00:05:35,368 --> 00:05:39,435
as a combination of the others, so it adds nothing new to the span.

89
00:05:40,035 --> 00:05:44,619
Combining span and linear independence gives us the notion of a basis.

90
00:05:44,619 --> 00:05:48,876
A basis of V is a set that is linearly independent and also spans

91
00:05:48,876 --> 00:05:52,412
V, so it describes the whole space with no redundancy.

92
00:05:52,412 --> 00:05:56,407
A key fact is that every basis of the same space has the same

93
00:05:56,407 --> 00:05:57,651
number of elements.

94
00:05:57,651 --> 00:06:01,318
That common number is called the dimension of the space.

95
00:06:01,318 --> 00:06:05,771
For example, the standard basis of R n consists of n vectors, so the

96
00:06:05,771 --> 00:06:09,962
dimension of R n is n, which matches our intuition that R two is

97
00:06:09,962 --> 00:06:12,450
a plane and R three is ordinary space.

98
00:06:13,050 --> 00:06:17,172
Now we move on to part 7: Exercise and solution.

99
00:06:17,772 --> 00:06:22,575
Having seen what a basis is and how dimension counts its vectors, let's put

100
00:06:22,575 --> 00:06:25,264
those ideas to work with a short exercise.

101
00:06:25,264 --> 00:06:29,171
Are the vectors one, two and three, six linearly independent?

102
00:06:29,171 --> 00:06:34,294
Look closely and you'll notice that the second vector is exactly three times the

103
00:06:34,294 --> 00:06:34,678
first.

104
00:06:34,678 --> 00:06:39,865
That observation gives us a relation right away: three times the vector one, two,

105
00:06:39,865 --> 00:06:43,963
minus the vector three, six, equals the zero vector, zero, zero.

106
00:06:43,963 --> 00:06:49,791
This is a nontrivial linear combination, because the coefficients, three and minus one, are

107
00:06:49,791 --> 00:06:53,121
not all zero, and yet the result is the zero vector.

108
00:06:53,121 --> 00:06:56,899
That is precisely what it means to prove linear dependence.

109
00:06:56,899 --> 00:06:59,717
So these two vectors are linearly dependent.

110
00:06:59,717 --> 00:07:05,160
Geometrically, both point along the same direction, so together they span only a line

111
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:10,219
through the origin, not the whole plane, and therefore they cannot form a basis

112
00:07:10,219 --> 00:07:10,667
for it.

113
00:07:11,267 --> 00:07:14,125
That brings us to the end of this lecture.

114
00:07:14,125 --> 00:07:20,115
We covered Introduction, Vectors in R n, Definition of a vector space, Subspaces, Linear

115
00:07:20,115 --> 00:07:25,491
combinations and span, Linear independence and basis and Exercise and solution.

116
00:07:25,491 --> 00:07:27,057
Thank you for watching.
